  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery reveals a gradually expanding \ncumulus field across the northern Great Basin as another plume \nof monsoonal moisture pushes into eastern Idaho. Similar to \nrecent days, another round of afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms is expected to develop across the forecast area. \nWhile overall thermodynamic and shear parameters are less \nfavorable for widespread severe convection than yesterday, an \nisolated severe storm cannot be entirely ruled out. The primary \nthreats with stronger cores will be localized gusty outflow \nwinds and small hail. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) \nmaintains a Marginal Risk for severe thunderstorms across the \nsouthern two-thirds of the forecast area through this evening, \nwith activity tapering off into the night.\n\nThe marginal severe threat is expected to subside by Tuesday \nmorning as atmospheric forcing weakens. From Tuesday through \nThursday, weak high-pressure ridging will rebuild across the \nregion, leading to diminishing precipitation chances and overall\nweaker thunderstorm intensity. While isolated diurnal showers \nand storms remain possible each afternoon, coverage will be far \nmore sparse. High pressure will also drive a warming trend, with\nafternoon maximum temperatures returning to the lower 90s \nacross lower-elevation valleys through Thursday.\n\nBy late Thursday and continuing into Friday, eastern Idaho will\nbegin feeling the influence of a prolonged upper-level trough\nestablishing itself across the Pacific Northwest. As this\nfeature interacts with the ridge to our south, the regional\nsurface pressure gradient will tighten significantly, generating\nincreasingly breezy conditions Friday through the weekend. In\naddition to the wind, incoming H5 height falls will suppress the\nGreat Basin ridge, initiating a cooling trend that will drop\nweekend temperatures to the cooler side of seasonal averages.\nLatest model guidance continues to introduce low-end\nprecipitation chances as energy moves around the trough axis.\nModel guidance still exhibits minor variance regarding the exact\ndepth and track of the core low as it shifts north of the\nregion, which will dictate final wind speeds and precipitation\ndistribution, but at least a modest cooldown remains high-\nconfidence for the weekend.",
